# SYNOPSIS

```usage
kill name ...
```

# DESCRIPTION

`kill` prints, for each process whose name is one of the <name>s, the rc command that kills it: an `echo kill` to its `note` in `/proc` (proc(4)), followed by a comment naming it.
It kills nothing itself; piping its output to rc does, as in Plan 9.

# EXAMPLES

```rc
% kill a
@{echo kill>/proc/5636/note} # a
% kill a | rc
```

# SEE ALSO

ps(1), proc(4), rc(1)

# BUGS

Plan 9's `kill` is an rc script over `sed`; this system has no `sed` yet, so it is a program that prints what the script would.
